# Effect + Alchemy peer patterns for rat-stack

These peer patterns guide the web client, Alchemy resources, database, Better Auth, Worker tests, and boundary enforcement.
The [peer roster](./peers) lists the candidates and links each studied repo here.
See [the layer-startups decision](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/.brain/projects/layer-startups.svx) for the choices they inform.

## Pin reality

The original study base, `1c2a738`, pinned Effect `4.0.0-rc.117`, Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, and XState `6.0.0-alpha.59`. Those are historical versions. `packages/core/test/docs-pins.test.ts` guards the version text in `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`. The [Effect reference note](./effect-4-reference-projects) records the versions on its 2026-09-18 study date.

At that study date, only `alchemy-run/alchemy` at `9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176` matched both Effect and Alchemy lines: its package is beta.79 and its workspace requires Effect rc.117. Maple is one Effect RC behind at rc.116 / Alchemy beta.79; T3Code is rc.115 / beta.79. OpenSEO is Effect beta.93 / Alchemy beta.61, SolStatus beta.107 / beta.72, Smithers rc.115 / beta.76, Pivot beta.103 / beta.65, and Elianiva beta.101 / beta.64. The roster includes repos on shared stack lines with different exact pins. Use older source to study shapes; check APIs before copying code.

The Oct 2 refresh found stable Effect 4 in Alchemy, effect-cf, and vitesakuga. Alchemy also moved to beta.80.

The Oct 9 refresh checks all 29 existing peers and adds 21 verified public projects. The [roster](./peers) records their commits, manifests, and tiers.
Alchemy moves to beta.81. Several peers use stable Effect 4.0.1 or 4.0.2. rat-stack keeps Effect 4.0.0 to satisfy Foldkit's exact peers.
Patterns below keep their original source snapshots and study dates.

## Patterns

### Browser RPC behind a private Worker binding — adopt

**Source:** [Alchemy example README](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/README.md), [RPC schema](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/src/backend/rpc.ts), [browser client](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/src/rpc-client.ts), [proxy route](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/src/routes/rpc.ts), [Stack](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/alchemy.run.ts).

One `RpcGroup` declares the wire contract; the backend serves it and `AtomRpc.Service` turns it into module-scope query and mutation atoms for React. The browser calls a same-origin TanStack `/rpc` route, which forwards to a private `BACKEND` service binding; the backend has no public Workers URL.

Adopt this for the web bin. Keep capability schemas in `packages/core`, the `toRpc` projection in `packages/capability`, and UI atoms in the web app.
One schema-derived contract carries payloads and typed failures across the wire. The [binding](/lore/bindings) keeps the backend private.
All browser operations go through `toRpc` and the same `/rpc` transport. Keep hand-written domain routes out.

### PlanetScale resources and migrations — adapt

**Source:** [T3Code relay database](https://github.com/pingdotgg/t3code/blob/f5ef0ddb90a8c36584e181b1913e7b8a5df30ffc/infra/relay/src/db.ts) at `f5ef0ddb90a8c36584e181b1913e7b8a5df30ffc` (Effect rc.115, Alchemy beta.79).

T3Code makes `Drizzle.Schema` the migration source, then provisions a PlanetScale database or stage branch, a runtime role, and a Hyperdrive connection from that role's origin. Production retains the shared database; non-production stages use branches. This is the closest inspected resource graph to rat-stack's planned PlanetScale Postgres Layer.

Adapt this for the database cartridge. The vendor Layer should own its schema, migration path, role, and Hyperdrive binding.
Use Alchemy Outputs and typed bindings for connection strings. Keep provider-specific Drizzle details behind the database service tag.
Choose D1 or PlanetScale once with `Layer.provide`. Keep T3Code's stage-mode branch out of domain code.

### Effect-native Drizzle and local Postgres tests — adapt

**Source:** Maple's [`packages/db/src/client.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/packages/db/src/client.ts), [`pglite.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/packages/db/src/pglite.ts), and [`migrate.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/packages/db/src/migrate.ts) at `e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373` (Effect rc.116, Alchemy beta.79).

Maple builds Drizzle over `@effect/sql-pg`, opens the client inside an Effect `Scope`, and uses PGlite plus the same bundled migrations for local and test paths. Its exported `MapleDb` remains Postgres-specific. Use the driver implementation and test technique behind a provider-neutral database contract.

Adapt this for the database cartridge. Keep the public repository-shaped database service small and let each vendor Layer implement it.
Scope clients to one invocation. Use a real embedded database for migrations and query tests. Keep Drizzle's driver type behind the cross-provider API.
Share migration fixtures and the service contract. D1 and Postgres keep their own schemas and implementations, as Joel decided.

### Better Auth's database seam — adopt

**Source:** Alchemy's [`Database.ts`](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/packages/better-auth/src/Database.ts), [`CloudflareD1.ts`](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/packages/better-auth/src/CloudflareD1.ts), [`CloudflareHyperdrive.ts`](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/packages/better-auth/src/CloudflareHyperdrive.ts), [`BetterAuth.ts`](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/packages/better-auth/src/BetterAuth.ts), and [example Worker](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-better-auth/src/worker.ts).

`@alchemy.run/better-auth@2.0.0-beta.79` makes `Database` a service supplied by provider-specific Layers, and `BetterAuth` consumes that service. D1 uses a runtime Worker binding and a separate deploy-time migration connection; Hyperdrive accepts the origin URL for migrations because its runtime connection is minted per invocation, and the integration scopes auth/database work to that execution.

Adopt the official Better Auth integration. It is a separately published Alchemy package with provider subpaths, so rat-stack can use it without forking an adapter.
Its API and migration failures are typed. Database clients are scoped, and the caller supplies one database Layer.
Use one `BetterAuth` service with the database cartridge's selected Layer. Keep runtime D1/Postgres switching out of auth code.

### Runtime database-provider switching — reject

**Source:** OpenSEO's [`auth.ts`](https://github.com/every-app/open-seo/blob/0ffff93101043aad7600a3b6a499a0cd2887ef49/src/lib/auth.ts), [`provider.ts`](https://github.com/every-app/open-seo/blob/0ffff93101043aad7600a3b6a499a0cd2887ef49/src/db/provider.ts), [`pg/client.ts`](https://github.com/every-app/open-seo/blob/0ffff93101043aad7600a3b6a499a0cd2887ef49/src/db/pg/client.ts), and [`alchemy.run.ts`](https://github.com/every-app/open-seo/blob/0ffff93101043aad7600a3b6a499a0cd2887ef49/alchemy.run.ts) at `0ffff93101043aad7600a3b6a499a0cd2887ef49` (Effect beta.93, Alchemy beta.61).

OpenSEO constructs Better Auth directly and selects either a D1 Drizzle adapter or a Postgres adapter by reading `DATABASE_PROVIDER` at runtime. Its Postgres client is created inside an `AsyncLocalStorage` request scope and reaches the database through Hyperdrive, but the auth and schema code still branch on provider.

Reject the selector and retain the request-scope invariant. The branch ties auth to both database implementations; either database Layer should plug into one contract.
An environment string chooses the driver at runtime, outside the `Schema`/Layer boundary.
Keep the per-request Hyperdrive lifetime. Move provider choice to one `Layer.provide` at composition.

### Fast Worker and Durable Object tests — adopt

**Source:** Maple's [`worker-bridge.test.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/apps/api/src/worker-bridge.test.ts), [`ChatSessionObject.test.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/apps/ai/src/chat/ChatSessionObject.test.ts), and [`fake-do-state.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/apps/ai/test/chat/fake-do-state.ts), same Maple commit as above.

The bridge test invokes Alchemy's `Cloudflare.Workers.makeRequestHandler` and checks the request scope, Worker environment, and response. Durable Object tests give the class a narrow fake state backed by real `node:sqlite`, including SQL storage, alarms, and `waitUntil`; they exercise object behavior without pretending to be a full `workerd` runtime.

Adopt this for fast tests alongside real-runtime tests. Keep fixtures beside the Worker or database cartridge that owns them.
Real SQL catches query and storage drift. The narrow fake fails when tests ask for an unsupported state operation.
Use the Alchemy bridge for deterministic Worker tests. Keep a separate integration test for real service bindings and deployed behavior.

### D1 fixture backed by SQLite — adapt

**Source:** Smithers' [`worker/test/d1.ts`](https://github.com/smithersai/smithers/blob/38846c64dd322d61e67efafadac1fbd9d34df64f/packages/smithers/build/infra/worker/test/d1.ts) and [`action-cache.test.ts`](https://github.com/smithersai/smithers/blob/38846c64dd322d61e67efafadac1fbd9d34df64f/packages/smithers/build/infra/worker/test/action-cache.test.ts) at `38846c64dd322d61e67efafadac1fbd9d34df64f` (Effect rc.115, Alchemy beta.76).

The test adapter runs shipped SQL migrations against `node:sqlite`, implements only the D1 calls the code uses, and throws on every unsupported method. That makes it a useful D1 unit-test boundary, but it does not prove Cloudflare's D1 binding or runtime behavior.

Adapt this for D1 cartridge tests. Expose a test Layer or helper from the D1 package. Keep it local to D1 rather than creating a repo-wide mock database.
Unsupported calls fail loudly. Use this for local SQL behavior and the Alchemy integration gate for binding behavior.

### Deployed Alchemy integration tests — adopt as a separate gate

**Source:** Alchemy's [`test/integ.test.ts`](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176/examples/cloudflare-tanstack-rpc-drizzle/test/integ.test.ts) at `9802260db8f8b5a083c4b85eb7ea31ae1ff8f176`.

The example's `alchemy/Test/Bun` harness deploys the Stack before tests, retries readiness, sends CRUD calls through the `/rpc` proxy into the database, verifies a tagged failure crosses the wire, and destroys the Stack afterward. This is the closest inspected example of the real-resource integration path called for in `VISION.md`, but its Bun runner is not a fit for rat-stack's pnpm test stack.

Adapt this as a separate credentialed integration gate. Keep fast unit tests uncredentialed.
The integration suite belongs to the Stack and can be removed with it. It proves the full binding, wire, and database path.
Port the deploy/test/destroy lifecycle into a pnpm-compatible harness. Keep `alchemy/Test/Bun` out.

### Boundary tests and agent guidance — adapt

**Source:** Maple's [`vendor-isolation.test.ts`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/packages/chat-platform/src/vendor-isolation.test.ts) and [`CLAUDE.md`](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/maple/blob/e7bc2d97bea87c99c8b9b33f377956e923d04373/CLAUDE.md), same Maple commit.

The test scans explicitly named generic roots and files to keep chat-vendor names inside connector directories, and checks that guarded roots are non-empty and guarded files still exist. The guidance makes app, product-package, and vendor-neutral-library ownership explicit; it also says services capture implementation dependencies and keep request context invocation-scoped.

Adapt the proof. Maple's lexical vendor-name scanner is product-specific; rat-stack needs checks for its own package ownership and adapter isolation.
Encode the capability-only surface rule in a test or type-aware lint rule. Check that guarded paths exist and contain files.
Extend the current capability fence rather than adding a second generic source-scanning framework.

### Scoped actor inspection and generated model commands — adapt

**Source:** beep-effect at `a7644af785843bb42546109272970f2f7a82b1d7`: [catalog](https://github.com/beep-effect/beep-effect/blob/a7644af785843bb42546109272970f2f7a82b1d7/package.json), [driver manifest](https://github.com/beep-effect/beep-effect/blob/a7644af785843bb42546109272970f2f7a82b1d7/packages/drivers/xstate/package.json), [inspector service](https://github.com/beep-effect/beep-effect/blob/a7644af785843bb42546109272970f2f7a82b1d7/packages/drivers/xstate/src/StatelyInspector.service.ts), [test bridge](https://github.com/beep-effect/beep-effect/blob/a7644af785843bb42546109272970f2f7a82b1d7/packages/drivers/xstate/src/test.ts).

Checked Oct 2. Its catalog selects XState alpha.63 and bridge alpha.5. Effect comes from a pkg.pr.new build at `b5a2d4c1d62c9620a68d72b7f20248c69ef7663b`. That [source manifest](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/b5a2d4c1d62c9620a68d72b7f20248c69ef7663b/packages/effect/package.json) declares 4.0.0; the installed build's semver was not verified. It shares a stack line; an exact release match remains unverified.

The inspector has a disabled production Layer. Its enabled Layer owns the inspector, and `attach` acquires an actor subscription whose finalizer unsubscribes. The test bridge derives event arbitraries from schemas and projects a snapshot's value and context for model comparisons. Its actor SUT opens a scope and closes it on disposal.

Adapt the lifetime checks. Keep `watchActor` and `ActorWatch` in [devtools](/systems/devtools) as our single inspection path. A second Stately service would bypass that fence. The helper runs tests through `ManagedRuntime`, which our test fence forbids. Use `it.effect.prop` and generated command sequences instead of copying its runtime bridge. No production code was adopted.


### Verify Foldkit's exact Effect peers: adopt

**Source:** Hazel at `2504baa1ab85d7966152a615a08dc0d4af4c1f69`: [catalogs](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/hazel/blob/2504baa1ab85d7966152a615a08dc0d4af4c1f69/package.json), [Foldkit client](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/hazel/blob/2504baa1ab85d7966152a615a08dc0d4af4c1f69/apps/web-foldkit/package.json), [lockfile](https://github.com/MapleTechLabs/hazel/blob/2504baa1ab85d7966152a615a08dc0d4af4c1f69/bun.lock).

The upstream manifests at `49dc36ef672d1caee23f153f88c09d98c8241670` confirm the boundary: [Foldkit](https://github.com/foldkit/foldkit/blob/49dc36ef672d1caee23f153f88c09d98c8241670/packages/foldkit/package.json) and [Vite plugin](https://github.com/foldkit/foldkit/blob/49dc36ef672d1caee23f153f88c09d98c8241670/packages/vite-plugin-foldkit/package.json).

Checked Oct 9. Does the shared catalog satisfy Foldkit's exact Effect peers?

- The catalog selects Effect 4.0.1 and Alchemy beta.80.
- The client selects Foldkit 0.167.0 and uses that Effect catalog.
- The lockfile records Foldkit's exact Effect 4.0.0 peer.
- The Vite plugin also requires Effect 4.0.0 and depends on platform-node 4.0.0.

Adopt the manifest and lockfile check. Keep Effect 4.0.0 until Foldkit publishes compatible peers and platform dependencies.
Do not copy the newer Effect catalog. This study changes no runtime adapter or UI code.

### Template lifetime boundaries: adopt

**Source:** [architecture](https://github.com/just-be-dev/effect-cloudflare-foldkit-template/blob/04851db8649501bfaf8d2712429074ba0290a10f/docs/architecture.md) and [manifest](https://github.com/just-be-dev/effect-cloudflare-foldkit-template/blob/04851db8649501bfaf8d2712429074ba0290a10f/package.json) at `04851db8649501bfaf8d2712429074ba0290a10f`.

The source assigns each resource to an application, request, tenant, session, or execution. It acquires request capabilities at the request boundary.
rat-stack already adapts this guidance in [cache completed values](/lore/cache-completed-values), UI tests, and opt-in tracing. `PROVENANCE.md` records the source and license confirmation.

Keep those lifetime boundaries. The template uses Effect 4.0.0 and Alchemy beta.81, with Foldkit 0.165.0. Its package-manager defaults do not replace pnpm here.
This refresh records the existing adoption. It copies no new implementation.

## Other screened projects

`jonbeckman/solstatus` at `83b3e2f4400aa7a2f2f4b738334e0cf91dbf15f5` has Alchemy `Cloudflare.Website.Vite`, D1, and direct Better Auth, but pins Effect beta.107 / Alchemy beta.72; its runtime is too old to copy. `elianiva/elianiva.com` at `5799fcb6b5ddc48e1df0223f23a9a15da8d097ae` also has a Website.Vite Stack, but uses Effect beta.101 / Alchemy beta.64 and adds no stronger pattern than the current Alchemy example. `13kparkin/Pivot` at `1887824d1f96ea6b601fffbc283b836967d32a5c` shows PlanetScale/Hyperdrive but uses Effect beta.103 / Alchemy beta.65; T3Code's newer PlanetScale graph supersedes it for this decision.

Symbol-screened without a relevant client/resource pattern: `Dhravya/radish`, `zonko-ai/harbor-sdk`, `dmmulroy/overseer`, `instructa/planr`, and `statelyai/agent`. `cameronapak/dotflowy` has Better Auth but no Alchemy integration and uses Effect rc.115 / XState alpha.12, so it is not a useful match for this lane. The earlier Effect reference note already covers the XState bridge; this note does not repeat it.

## Recommendations from the September study

These recommendations record the original study. The current web bin uses Foldkit; [current pins](/pins.md) come from manifests.

1. TanStack web bin: generate the browser client from capability Schemas as one `RpcGroup`, create module-scope `AtomRpc` atoms, and forward same-origin `/rpc` to a private `RpcWorker` binding.
2. Better Auth: use `@alchemy.run/better-auth@2.0.0-beta.79`; provide the database cartridge's Layer and keep auth API/migration failures typed.
3. Database cartridge: expose one repository-shaped service tag; let D1 and PlanetScale/Hyperdrive Layers own schemas, migrations, and bindings; choose with one `Layer.provide`.
4. Worker tests: add `makeRequestHandler` and SQLite-backed DO/D1 fixtures for fast tests, plus one credentialed Alchemy deploy/test/destroy gate using a pnpm-compatible harness.
5. Capability fence: test or lint that every surface, including `toRpc`, originates from the capability registry; make the test prove its guarded paths exist and contain files.
